=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — "router4 is down" with the entire Location C office unable to reach its gateway or any remote site — is not corroborated by any evidence in the live lab. A thorough inspection of every relevant component shows the network is fully operational.

**Router4** is running correctly: the container is live, all three configured interfaces are up (eth0/192.168.200.1/24 serving the Location C LAN; eth4/192.168.15.2/24 toward router3; eth5/172.25.1.1/24 toward router5), FRR's RIPv2 daemon is active, and both WAN neighbors (router3 at 192.168.15.1 and router5 at 172.25.1.2) are exchanging updates with no bad-packet or bad-route counts. The RIP table carries a full set of routes to all remote subnets (10.16.1.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24, 10.50.1.0/24, 172.16.x.0/24, 172.20.1.0/24, 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.100.0/24). There are no iptables rules filtering any traffic on router4.

**The Location C LAN** is equally healthy: PC11 (192.168.200.11), PC12 (.12), PC13 (.13), and PC14 (.14) all have correct IPs and default gateway 192.168.200.1. Ping to the gateway succeeds from all four hosts with sub-2 ms RTT. From PC11, pings to twelve distinct destinations across every remote subnet all succeed with 0% loss. switch4, switch5, and switch6 all show their bridge ports UP and forwarding.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The ticket appears to be based on a false premise — either a transient glitch that resolved before the investigation began, or a mistaken report. No corrective action is required at this time.
=== END ===